# moodle-ci-workflows

Central, reusable GitHub Actions workflows for the Moodle plugins of
[bycs-lp](https://github.com/bycs-lp).

Instead of maintaining the same lengthy workflow files in every plugin repository,
each plugin just calls these reusable workflows and only overrides the settings it
actually needs.

| Workflow | Purpose |
| --- | --- |
| [`moodle-plugin-ci.yml`](.github/workflows/moodle-plugin-ci.yml) | Runs the moodle-plugin-ci checks (lint, phpcs, PHPUnit, Behat, ...) |
| [`moodle-release.yml`](.github/workflows/moodle-release.yml) | Publishes a released version to the Moodle Marketplace |

## Referencing: always `@main`

The plugins deliberately reference the workflows via `@main`:

```yaml
uses: bycs-lp/moodle-ci-workflows/.github/workflows/moodle-plugin-ci.yml@main
```

That is the whole point of this repository: a change made here (new Moodle branches,
a new PHP version, updated database images, an additional check) takes effect
immediately in **all** plugin repositories. No tag bump, no bulk pull request across
15 repositories.

Consequence: **changes on `main` go live immediately.** Develop non-trivial changes on
a branch and test them from a plugin against that branch
(`...moodle-plugin-ci.yml@my-branch`) before merging into `main`.

Inputs may be renamed or removed whenever it makes the configuration cleaner. There is
no backwards compatibility to maintain, the calling workflows in the plugins are simply
updated in the same go.

---

# CI workflow

## Minimal example

`.github/workflows/ci.yml` in the plugin repository:

```yaml
name: Moodle Plugin CI

on: [push, pull_request]

jobs:
  ci:
    uses: bycs-lp/moodle-ci-workflows/.github/workflows/moodle-plugin-ci.yml@main
```

This runs the complete default configuration: all checks, and the Moodle branches
derived from `$plugin->supported` in the plugin's `version.php`.

## Example with customisations

```yaml
name: Moodle Plugin CI

on:
  push:
    branches: [main, MOODLE_500_STABLE]
  pull_request:

jobs:
  ci:
    uses: bycs-lp/moodle-ci-workflows/.github/workflows/moodle-plugin-ci.yml@main
    with:
      # Only test these PHP versions.
      php-versions: '["8.3", "8.4"]'
      databases: '["pgsql"]'
      # Opt out of single checks (everything runs by default).
      run-grunt: false
      # The mustache lint must not fail the build.
      mustache-continue-on-error: true
```

## Inputs

### Matrix

| Input | Type | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `php-versions` | string (JSON array) | `["8.3", "8.4"]` | PHP versions to test (manually selected, then filtered per Moodle branch compatibility) |
| `moodle-branches` | string (JSON array) | `''` | Explicit list of Moodle branches. Empty means: derive them automatically, see below. |
| `exclude-moodle-branches` | string (JSON array) | `[]` | Branch names to drop from the resolved list |
| `databases` | string (JSON array) | `["pgsql", "mariadb"]` | Databases (`pgsql`, `mariadb`, `mysqli`) |
| `matrix-exclude` | string (JSON array) | `[]` | Combinations to exclude |
| `matrix-include` | string (JSON array) | `[]` | Additional combinations |

> **Note:** GitHub Actions does not allow list inputs for matrix axes, so these are
> passed as JSON strings and read with `fromJSON()` inside the workflow.

### Moodle branches are resolved automatically

Nothing is hardcoded. The **Resolve test matrix** job works out the branches at runtime:

1. `$plugin->supported` is read from the plugin's `version.php`. It is a
   `[min, max]` pair of Moodle branch numbers, for example `[405, 502]`.
2. The list of stable branches that actually exist is queried from
   `github.com/moodle/moodle`, and everything within the supported range is selected.
3. The development branch `main` is always added.
4. Everything listed in `exclude-moodle-branches` is dropped.

So `$plugin->supported = [500, 502]` results in:

```text
["main", "MOODLE_500_STABLE", "MOODLE_501_STABLE", "MOODLE_502_STABLE"]
```

The practical effect: when a plugin starts supporting a new Moodle major release, the
only change needed is bumping `supported` in `version.php`. Neither the plugin's
`ci.yml` nor this repository has to be touched. The resolved list is written to the
job summary of every run.

Single branches can be dropped without hardcoding the whole list, including `main`:

```yaml
with:
  exclude-moodle-branches: '["main", "MOODLE_405_STABLE"]'
```

If a plugin really needs a fixed list, `moodle-branches` overrides the whole
resolution:

```yaml
with:
  moodle-branches: '["main", "MOODLE_502_STABLE"]'
```

The workflow fails with an explicit error if `version.php` has no `supported`
attribute and no `moodle-branches` input is given, or if all branches end up excluded.

### PHP versions are chosen manually and filtered per branch

`php-versions` stays a deliberate, manual choice. A plugin should test a small, focused
set of PHP versions, not every version that would theoretically work.

What the workflow adds is only the negative case: a configured PHP version that the
target Moodle branch cannot run is dropped for that branch. The limits come from Moodle
core's `public/admin/environment.xml`, which describes every release in one file
(before Moodle 5.0 the file lived in `admin/environment.xml`, both paths are tried):

```xml
<MOODLE version="5.2">                            <!-- release          -->
  <PHP version="8.3.0">                           <!-- minimum PHP      -->
    <RESTRICT function="restrict_php_version_84"> <!-- PHP >= 8.4 fails -->
```

So `php-versions: '["8.1", "8.2", "8.3", "8.4"]'` becomes:

| Moodle branch | Supported PHP | Tested PHP |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `main` | unknown | 8.1, 8.2, 8.3, 8.4 |
| `MOODLE_502_STABLE` | >= 8.3.0 | 8.3, 8.4 |
| `MOODLE_405_STABLE` | >= 8.1.0, < 8.4 | 8.1, 8.2, 8.3 |

The same table is written to the job summary of every run. If no configured PHP version
is left for a branch, that branch is skipped with a warning. `matrix-include` entries
that pin an impossible `php` / `moodle-branch` pair are dropped as well.

`main` has no release entry in `environment.xml`, so it is never filtered.

### The matrix resolution is a plain shell script

The logic lives in [`scripts/resolve-matrix.sh`](scripts/resolve-matrix.sh) instead of
being buried in the workflow YAML, so it can be read, changed and run on its own. It
needs nothing but `bash`, `curl`, `awk` and `jq`, all of which are present on the GitHub
runners.

```bash
MOODLE_BRANCHES='["main","MOODLE_405_STABLE","MOODLE_502_STABLE"]' \
PHP_VERSIONS='["8.1","8.2","8.3","8.4"]' \
DATABASES='["pgsql"]' \
./scripts/resolve-matrix.sh
```

`ENVIRONMENT_XML` can point at a local `environment.xml` instead of the copy in Moodle
core, which makes it possible to try the script out without network access.

The workflow checks this repository out into `ci-workflows/` before it runs the script.
The ref used for that is `github.job_workflow_sha`, the commit of the reusable workflow
file itself, so the script always matches the workflow that is running, no matter which
plugin repository triggered it and no matter whether it was referenced via `@main` or
via a feature branch.

> Do not use `github.workflow_ref` here. Inside a reusable workflow the whole `github`
> context describes the *caller*, so that value points at the plugin repository, not at
> this one.

> **Keep this repository public.** The checkout of the scripts uses the automatic
> `GITHUB_TOKEN` of the calling plugin repository, which may read public repositories
> only. Making this repository private would break every plugin CI run and would
> require passing an additional token.

### Environment

| Input | Type | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `runs-on` | string | `ubuntu-latest` | Runner image |
| `postgres-image` | string | `postgres:latest` | Docker image for PostgreSQL |
| `mariadb-image` | string | `mariadb:latest` | Docker image for MariaDB |
| `moodle-plugin-ci-version` | string | `^4` | Composer constraint for `moodlehq/moodle-plugin-ci` |
| `node-version` | string | `''` | Optional explicit Node.js version |
| `extra-plugins` | string | `''` | Newline separated list of dependent plugins, see below |
| `install-extra-args` | string | `''` | Additional arguments for `moodle-plugin-ci install` |

### Check toggles (opt-out)

By default **all** moodle-plugin-ci checks run. A plugin only has to switch off the
checks it does not need.

| Input | Default |
| --- | --- |
| `run-phplint` | `true` |
| `run-phpcs` | `true` |
| `run-validate` | `true` |
| `run-savepoints` | `true` |
| `run-mustache` | `true` |
| `run-grunt` | `true` |
| `run-phpunit` | `true` |
| `run-subplugin-phpunit` | `true` |
| `run-behat` | `true` |

Example of an opt-out:

```yaml
with:
  run-grunt: false
  run-behat: false
```

> There is no separate PHPDoc check. `moodle-plugin-ci phpdoc` has been dropped on
> purpose, because those checks are covered by `phpcs`.

> `run-subplugin-phpunit` runs PHPUnit for every subplugin listed in
> `db/subplugins.json` and is skipped automatically if the plugin has none.

### Fine tuning

| Input | Type | Default |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `phpcs-max-warnings` | number | `0` |
| `grunt-max-lint-warnings` | number | `0` |
| `mustache-continue-on-error` | boolean | `false` |
| `phpunit-continue-on-error` | boolean | `false` |
| `behat-profile` | string | `chrome` |

## Dependent plugins

Plugins such as `tiny_ai` need further plugins (for example `local_ai_manager`) to be
installable. They are listed in `extra-plugins`, one per line, and are cloned into
`EXTRA_PLUGINS_DIR` automatically.

Every line is either a GitHub shorthand (`owner/repo`) or a full git URL. Both forms
accept an optional `@<ref>` suffix to pin a branch or tag:

```yaml
with:
  extra-plugins: |
    bycs-lp/moodle-local_ai_manager
    bycs-lp/moodle-local_ai_manager@MOODLE_500_STABLE
    https://github.com/bycs-lp/moodle-local_ai_manager.git
    https://gitlab.example.org/group/moodle-local_something.git@main
    git://example.org/moodle-local_something.git
    git@github.com:bycs-lp/moodle-local_ai_manager.git@main
```

Anything containing `://` or starting with `git@` is used as the clone URL verbatim,
everything else is expanded to `https://github.com/<owner>/<repo>.git`. The `@<ref>`
suffix is only recognised if the part after the last `@` contains neither `/` nor `:`,
so SSH URLs like `git@github.com:owner/repo.git` are not misinterpreted.

Note that SSH URLs require a matching deploy key or SSH agent on the runner, and that
GitHub no longer supports the unauthenticated `git://` protocol. For public
repositories `https://` is the safe choice.

---

# Release workflow

`moodle-release.yml` publishes a released plugin version to the
[Moodle Marketplace](https://marketplace.moodle.com/). It is a thin wrapper around the
official workflow of Moodle HQ,
[moodlehq/moodle-plugin-release](https://github.com/moodlehq/moodle-plugin-release).

The wrapper exists so that the upstream reference and any future deviations are
maintained in one place. If Moodle HQ changes something that does not fit us, or the
upstream workflow breaks, we can pin a known good commit SHA or replace the job by our
own implementation here, without touching a single plugin repository.

## Usage

`.github/workflows/moodle-release.yml` in the plugin repository:

```yaml
name: Moodle Plugin Release

on:
  release:
    types: [published]

jobs:
  release:
    uses: bycs-lp/moodle-ci-workflows/.github/workflows/moodle-release.yml@main
    with:
      tag: ${{ github.event.release.tag_name }}
    secrets: inherit
```

## Setup

The API token is provided centrally as an organisation secret of `bycs-lp`, so nothing
has to be configured in the individual plugin repositories. Passing `secrets: inherit`
in the caller workflow is enough.

Publish a GitHub release and the workflow builds the zip package and submits it to the
marketplace.

## Inputs

| Input | Type | Default | Description |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| `tag` | string | `''` | Tag to release, e.g. `v1.4.0`. If empty, the upstream workflow derives it from `GITHUB_REF`. Passing it explicitly is recommended. |

| Secret | Required | Description |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `MOODLE_MARKETPLACE_TOKEN` | yes | Marketplace API token, provided as an organisation secret and passed on with `secrets: inherit`. |

## Notes

- The plugin component does **not** have to be configured. The upstream workflow reads
  `$plugin->component` from `version.php`.
- The description of the GitHub release is submitted as the release notes, so it is
  worth writing a proper one.

## Examples

Ready to use example configurations live in [`examples/`](examples/).



